# RiteMark Patch: browser-action-bridge
#
# Purpose: Expose Playwright-driven browser action commands (navigate, click,
#          fill, type, scroll, snapshot) and a dedicated AI-control consent
#          flag on BrowserViewModel, so the Ritemark extension host can drive
#          the integrated browser through AI agents (Sprint 69). Also registers
#          a workbench-level editor resolver for .html files so they open
#          directly in BrowserEditor with zero text-tab flicker.
#          Sprint 78: adds agentSnapshot (read-only ARIA outline, no consent).
#
# Sprint 135 (VS Code 1.142.0): revoking control when the share goes moves into
#   009's audience listener; the .html/.htm resolver uses getOrCreateLazy and
#   its own label key (ritemark.browser.htmlEditorLabel), beside upstream's new
#   .html entry, which stays (Ritemark's has default priority and wins); the
#   actions take RITEMARK_PLAYWRIGHT_SESSION.
#   The bar, as the Experience lead set it (D5): back, forward, reload, the
#   address, Show Claude this page, ⋯. 1.142's Add Element to Chat split button
#   is left off the bar even with Copilot Chat installed (it sends to Copilot
#   Chat, not to the AI beside the page; its command and shortcut stay), and the
#   empty tab's subtitle no longer points at it. Developer tools moves into ⋯
#   (hidden by default, which the bar puts in its ⋯ menu), in sentence case.
#
# #658 (2026-11-05): the turn's page is the one source for every action. Each
#   action takes the page by id (pageId) and acts in it, never again in the tab in
#   front, so a popup or another desk brought forward during the turn no longer
#   takes the AI's clicks and keys; a page that has gone is an error, never the
#   active tab. Navigate with an address loads into the named page or opens a new
#   tab (newTab); its fallbacks to the first visible browser pane and the first
#   browser tab are gone. A page the AI names itself (requireShared) must be shared
#   already: no action shares a page the AI was not given. Results list the
#   window's page ids and the pages new since the caller's knownPageIds (title and
#   address only when shared). An action never moves what the person sees:
#   no tab is brought forward. An input action (click, fill, type, key, scroll,
#   navigate into an existing page) on a page that is not in front is refused at
#   once ("The page isn't in front"), before any question; reads work on a hidden
#   page. The one exception is a navigate that opens a new tab
#   (the AI has no page): it cannot open in the background, because sharing it
#   asks the person a question about a tab they would not see. The first summary
#   of a new tab waits for the load and retries once. The control question
#   names the page. Logic in common/ritemarkBrowserPageTarget.ts, tested in
#   test/common/; the action path in test/electron-browser/features/.
#
# File(s): src/vs/platform/browserView/common/browserView.ts,
#          src/vs/workbench/contrib/browserView/common/browserView.ts,
#          src/vs/workbench/contrib/browserView/common/ritemarkBrowserPageTarget.ts,
#          src/vs/workbench/contrib/browserView/test/common/ritemarkBrowserPageTarget.test.ts,
#          src/vs/workbench/contrib/browserView/electron-browser/browserView.contribution.ts,
#          src/vs/workbench/contrib/browserView/electron-browser/features/ritemarkBrowserActionFeature.ts,
#          src/vs/workbench/contrib/browserView/electron-browser/features/browserEditorChatFeatures.ts,
#          src/vs/workbench/contrib/browserView/electron-browser/features/browserDevToolsFeature.ts,
#          src/vs/workbench/contrib/browserView/electron-browser/features/browserWelcomeFeature.ts,
#          src/vs/workbench/contrib/browserView/test/electron-browser/features/ritemarkBrowserActionFeature.test.ts
# Created: 2026-05-13
# Updated: 2026-11-05 (#658: actions act in the page named by id, never moving what the person sees)
#
